1. The prologue and calls to malloc/memset remain essentially the same (using x‑registers).

2. Then we set up an SVE predicate (“ptrue p0.s”) so that we can work on 32‑bit (“.s”) elements.

3. We use ld1w to load a vector from the first buffer. Next the SVE MUL (immediate) (“mul z0.s, z0.s, #3”) multiplies every element by 3. Following that, “and z0.s, z0.s, #0xFF” “ANDs” each element with 0xFF. (Recall that the immediate for AND is required to be a single run of ones repeated.)

4. The vector is then stored into the second buffer with st1w.

5. An ADDV reduces the vector to a single integer sum (placed in w1) which is then passed (via x1) along with the format string (in x0) to printf.

