One correct fix was to notice that the code “saves” a predicate register using a store‐instruction that writes VL/8 bytes. The manual’s comment “reserve a 16‐byte slot” is only true when the SVE vector length (VL) is 128 (so VL/8 = 16). However many implementations (and QEMU’s default SVE configuration) use a larger VL (for example, 256 bits so that VL/8 = 32). In that situation the simple “sub sp, sp, #16” does not reserve enough room and the predicate store (and later the temporary vector store) will smash other data (in fact the nearly‐adjacent frame pointer data) causing a segfault.

One safe fix is to reserve enough space on the stack for both a predicate backup and for temporary vector storage. For example, if we assume VL is 256 bits then the predicate register requires 32 bytes and an SVE vector register (Z0) will also be 32 bytes. (In general the amount written by str p0 and st1w { z0.s } is VL/8 bytes.) Thus we should reserve 32 bytes for the predicate backup and a further 32 bytes for our temporary storage.

Below is one complete version of the corrected assembly code. (Any solution that makes the appropriate adjustments is acceptable.) 

