Explanation:

• The “sub sp, sp, #64” reserves 64 bytes on the stack instead of only 16.
   – The lower 32 bytes (at [sp]) are used for storing the predicate register (p0) – note that with VL = 256 bits this is 32 bytes.
   – The upper 32 bytes (starting at sp + 32) are used to temporarily store the SVE vector (z0).

• The “mov x9, sp; add x9, x9, #32” sets x9 to point to the temporary vector storage.
• The load “ldr w0, [sp, #32]” picks up the first 32‐bit element from that stored vector.

