Below is one acceptable corrected solution. The idea is that you must not “hard‐code” 32 bytes for saving the predicate register and the Z register result because in SVE the register size is “scalable” (for example, if VL is 128 instead of 256 bits the size is 16 bytes, etc.). In the solution below we first use a predicate (with .b) to count the number of bytes in a predicate register (which is the same as the size of a Z register) and then we allocate 2×that many bytes on the stack. (We save p0 in the first “slot” and use the second slot for the temporary storage of z0.) Then we use a register‐computed add to get the proper offset. (Notice that the SVE load/store instructions for predicate registers use an immediate that is scaled by the predicate size—so if you want an offset of one “slot” you must add 1×(VL/8) bytes, not 32.)

One correct answer is shown below. (It does assume that the standard library function printf is available.)

<code>
		.text
		.global	main
		.type	main, %function
main:
		// Prologue: save FP and LR.
		stp		x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
		mov		x29, sp

		// Set up SVE predicate registers.
		// p0 will be used for our math (32‐bit lanes),
		// and p1 (as .b) will be used to count the number of bytes.
		ptrue	p0.s           // p0: all active lanes (for 32‐bit elements)
		ptrue	p1.b           // p1: all active lanes (as bytes)

		// Count the number of bytes in the current SVE vector (and predicate).
		// (This is the “scalable” size – e.g. 32 bytes if VL==256, or 16 if VL==128.)
		cntb	x19, p1        // x19 = size in bytes

		// We need two “slots”: one for saving the predicate, one for our temporary Z result.
		add		x20, x19, x19  // x20 = 2 * (current vector length in bytes)

		// Reserve the needed space on the stack.
		// (Because general‐purpose arithmetic uses byte offsets, we subtract the total size.)
		sub		sp, sp, x20

		// Save the predicate (p0) into the first slot.
		// (The predicate store instruction always scales its immediate offset by the predicate register size.)
		str		p0, [sp]
		// Reload the saved predicate into p2 so that we use it later in our math.
		ldr		p2, [sp]

		// The temporary area for z0 is immediately after the saved predicate.
		// Compute its address: x9 = sp + (size of one predicate register)
		add		x9, sp, x19

		// Now do the computation on each 32‐bit lane:
		//    result = ((100 – 1) ÷ 2 & 0xff) * 3
		dup		z0.s, #100			// z0 = {100, 100, …}
		sub		z0.s, z0.s, #1		// z0 = {99, 99, …}
		dup		z1.s, #2			// z1 = {2, 2, …}
		udiv	z0.s, p2/m, z0.s, z1.s	// udiv: 99 ÷ 2 = 49 on each active lane
		and		z0.s, z0.s, #0xff	// mask with 0xff (49 remains 49)
		mul		z0.s, z0.s, #3		// multiply: 49 * 3 = 147

		// Store the computed vector into temporary storage and then extract one result.
		st1w	{ z0.s }, p0, [x9]	// store the full vector to temporary storage at x9
		ldr		w0, [x9]		// load the first 32‐bit word of the result

		// Call printf: x0 = address of format string, w1 = computed integer.
		mov		w1, w0
		adrp	x0, fmt_str
		add		x0, x0, :lo12:fmt_str
		bl		printf

		// Epilogue: restore the stack and then the FP/LR.
		add		sp, sp, x20	// deallocate the SVE temporary area
		ldp		x29, x30, [sp], #32
		ret

		// Read‐only data: the format string for printf.
		.section	.rodata
fmt_str:
		.asciz	"%d\n"
</code>

A few comments:

1. We use ptrue with two different element sizes (p0.s and p1.b) so that we can count the number of bytes (with cntb) without affecting the predicate we use for arithmetic.

2. The stack frame is allocated dynamically based on the SVE “vector length” (VL); that is, the saved p0 occupies x19 bytes and the temporary Z storage occupies the next x19 bytes.

3. When storing/restoring a predicate register (with str/ldr for p‐registers) the immediate offset is interpreted in units of the predicate size; so an offset of 0 means “start here” and an offset of 1 would mean “p0 + (VL/8) bytes.”

This code compiles and links directly (calling the standard printf) and avoids the segfault by using the proper (scalable) offsets.

